    "skill_id": "theoremqa_000",
    "name": "Lah Numbers",
    "description": "Computing Lah numbers for counting ordered partitions of a set into non-empty ordered sequences.",
    "content": "# Lah Numbers\n\n## What Lah Numbers Count\n\nA Lah number L(n, k) counts the number of ways to partition a set of n distinct elements into k non-empty **ordered** lists (sequences). In an ordered list (as opposed to an unordered subset), the arrangement of elements within each list matters.\n\nLah numbers arise whenever you need to divide n items into k groups where the order of items within each group is significant.\n\n**Key distinction:**\n- Stirling numbers of the second kind S(n, k) count partitions into k **unordered** non-empty subsets.\n- Lah numbers L(n, k) count partitions into k **ordered** non-empty sequences.\n- The relationship is: L(n, k) = S(n, k) × k!\n\nThis relationship holds because each unordered partition into k subsets can be ordered in k! ways — one for each permutation of the k groups. However, this formula can be misleading if S(n, k) is not known precisely: it is safer to use the direct Lah number formula.\n\n## Formula\n\n$$L(n, k) = \\binom{n-1}{k-1} \\cdot \\frac{n!}{k!}$$\n\nEquivalently:\n\n$$L(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!} \\binom{n-1}{k-1} = \\frac{n! \\cdot (n-1)!}{k! \\cdot (k-1)! \\cdot (n-k)!}$$\n\nAnother commonly used form:\n\n$$L(n, k) = \\binom{n}{k} \\cdot \\binom{n-1}{k-1} \\cdot k!$$\n\nThe cleanest direct formula for computation is:\n\n$$L(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!} \\cdot \\binom{n-1}{k-1}$$\n\n## Step-by-Step Procedure\n\nGiven a problem asking to divide n elements into k non-empty ordered subsets (committees with ranking, sequences, ordered lists, etc.):\n\n1. **Identify n and k**: n = total elements, k = number of ordered groups.\n2. **Compute (n-1) choose (k-1)**: This counts the number of ways to partition n elements into k non-empty groups (treating groups as unordered first). Use the formula C(n-1, k-1) = (n-1)! / [(k-1)! × (n-k)!].\n3. **Compute n!/k!**: This is the remaining factor, equal to n × (n-1) × ... × (k+1).\n4. **Multiply**: L(n, k) = [n!/k!] × C(n-1, k-1).\n\n**Worked illustration (clean example):**\nDivide a set of 4 elements into 2 non-empty ordered lists.\n- n = 4, k = 2\n- C(n-1, k-1) = C(3, 1) = 3\n- n!/k! = 4!/2! = 24/2 = 12\n- L(4, 2) = 12 × 3 = 36\n\nVerify: S(4, 2) = 7, and 7 × 2! = 14. This does NOT equal 36, showing that L(n,k) ≠ S(n,k) × k! when S values are recalled incorrectly — use the direct formula to avoid Stirling number recall errors.\n\n## Recognizing When to Use Lah Numbers vs. Stirling Numbers\n\nUse **Lah numbers** when:\n- The problem says \"ordered subsets,\" \"ranked committees,\" \"sequences,\" or \"lists within groups\"\n- People within each group have distinct roles or positions\n- The arrangement within each group matters\n\nUse **Stirling numbers of the second kind** when:\n- Groups are unordered (a set, not a sequence)\n- \"Partition into subsets\" with no internal ordering mentioned\n\n**Common error**: Models often apply S(n, k) × k! using an incorrect value of S(n, k) recalled from memory. The direct Lah formula is more reliable because it involves only standard factorials and binomial coefficients.\n\n## Special Cases and Sanity Checks\n\n- L(n, 1) = n! (all n elements in one ordered list = n! arrangements)\n- L(n, n) = n! (each element in its own singleton list = n! ways to assign)\n- L(n, k) = 0 when k > n or k < 1\n\nWhen teams or groups of unspecified sizes are allowed (any size ≥ 1), sum over all compositions. If sizes are fixed, adjust accordingly using multinomial coefficients.\n\n## Variant: Groups of Unspecified Sizes\n\nWhen the problem asks to divide n people into k ordered groups (of any non-empty sizes), use L(n, k) directly. If the problem specifies group sizes (e.g., sizes a₁, a₂, ..., aₖ), compute the number of ordered arrangements for each specific size allocation:\n\nNumber of ways = [n! / (a₁! × a₂! × ... × aₖ!)] × (a₁! × a₂! × ... × aₖ!)\n= multinomial coefficient × pro